elaine: Quick sync — we need a single, concrete recommendation for `fetch` usage in the JS/TS SDK docs and sample apps. Users keep tripping over ESM/CJS packaging, edge runtime behavior, and missing typings. Any strong opinions?

matt: I vote for a tiny adapter we ship with the SDK: pick globalThis.fetch when available, fall back to undici in Node, and document the edge caveats. Keeps apps simple.

jess: Agree. Two big pain points I keep seeing: (1) TS projects missing lib.dom so Fetch types are unknown, and (2) requiring `undici` directly in ESM bundles causes bundle size / rebuild surprises for some customers.

anya: Also note runtime differences — Cloudflare/Vercel edge are web-fetch-y (Headers, Request, Response are web impls), Node fetch (Node 18+) is similar but some subtle runtime behavior differs (streaming, abort signal propagation). We should list the runtimes we test against.

elaine: Proposed minimal guidance to add to README / quickstart: 1) use the SDK's default `fetch` adapter (we provide it); 2) if you run in Node <18, install `undici` and our adapter will use it automatically; 3) for edge deployments, rely on native fetch and file a bug if something fails.

matt: +1. Can we show an example adapter users can copy? Something like:```js
// src/fetch-adapter.ts
import undici from 'undici';
const nativeFetch = (globalThis as any).fetch;
const nodeFetch = undici && (undici as any).fetch ? (undici as any).fetch : undefined;
const selected = nativeFetch ?? nodeFetch ?? (() => { throw new Error('No fetch available: install undici or run on a runtime with fetch'); });
export default function fetchAdapter(input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) {
  return selected(input as any, init as any);
}
```

jess: Couple of small notes to surface in docs: - recommend adding "lib": ["es2021", "dom"] to tsconfig to get fetch/Headers typings - show how to add types for undici if they install it (npm i -D @types/undici or the types shipped with the package)

anya: And packaging note: for our SDK package.json we should keep conditional exports so CJS consumers still work:```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
  }
}
```

ci-bot: build #2314 finished — all packaging tests passed on Node 18/20, esm/cjs bundles.

matt: One more runtime gotcha: AbortController signals sometimes behave differently between runtimes. Recommend we expose our own `makeAbortSignal` helper in the SDK docs with guidance to avoid reusing signals across retries.

elaine: Action items: (1) add small fetch adapter to SDK (drop-in), (2) update quickstart + sample-app to demonstrate Node <18 vs Edge, (3) docs blurb on tsconfig "dom" lib + conditional exports + AbortController note, (4) add a small test matrix (Node 18/20, Cloudflare, Vercel Edge). I'll open a PR with a draft README patch today.

jess: Happy to pair on the sample app changes. Quick checklist we can put in the PR description: - example tsconfig - snippet showing optional `npm i undici` - link to our adapter - compatibility table

anya: I can add the runtime test jobs once the adapter lands. Also propose a short note about server-side streaming caveats for edge vs node (we should not promise identical stream primitives).

matt: Sounds good. If the adapter is simple we can backport to older minor releases to help customers.

elaine: Thanks all. I'll open the PR and ping this thread. If anyone wants to add things to the PR checklist, drop them here. :+1:
